{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"4d1e1ce7dbbdf7fe881839de5c77d18756ff84a087ffe6a34bcc326c4c94b560","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_1eb474529f723a1604b0b75d633c593cb82bb7e078083810ad684a79a6017b28","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a coherent theoretical framework linking CNN invertibility to model-based compressive sensing, supported by empirical validation on VGGNet. The methodological soundness is high, with clear mathematical definitions and proofs. Experimental design is adequate for validating theoretical claims, though statistical rigor is limited by the lack of variance reporting. Reproducibility is supported by detailed hyperparameters and algorithm descriptions, though code availability is not explicitly stated.","paper_id":"B1mAJI9gl","paper_identity_sha256":"fe817678df997c7616004397772c0687d1c53a2a00e9a282bfdcefe79303b09e","prompt_identity_sha256":"5bd6de0a3084aa779f380b10500f9c3642e92fe0fe23a245888e7c44a817668c","raw_response_sha256":"3ca39365de4079479811582f3ecb5d8090c8c3e17d2601f622a5b0d3647585f5","request_identity_sha256":"503da5dd3930d13914be78002ef9299807ac6df71cf0f0a1ab36cf952927a801","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"ae94a46385b13489f484c6ce7dc8190db552b3ac4dad866f72426b7dc5e17d5d","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-018","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and well-formulated. The authors establish a clear mathematical connection between CNN layers and Iterative Hard Thresholding (IHT) for model-based compressive sensing. The definitions of model-sparsity and model-RIP are precise, and the theoretical bounds for reconstruction error are derived rigorously. The assumption that one iteration of IHT corresponds to a CNN layer is a strong conceptual contribution that aligns with the architecture's operations (convolution, ReLU, pooling). No major conceptual or implementation errors are apparent in the theoretical derivation.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-027","SRC-030","SRC-034","SRC-038"],"review_text":"The experimental design is appropriate for validating the theoretical claims. The authors use VGGNet on the ILSVRC-2012 dataset, a standard benchmark for deep CNNs, and evaluate both random and learned filters. The inclusion of CIFAR-10 experiments for random filter CNNs provides additional context for the effectiveness of random weights. The evaluation of model-RIP values and reconstruction errors across multiple layers is comprehensive. However, the experiments focus primarily on a single network architecture (VGGNet), which may limit the generalizability of the findings to other CNN architectures.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-038","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproducibility, including hyperparameters such as filter sizes, number of channels, and sparsity levels. The use of Caffe and MatConvNet toolboxes is specified, and the algorithms (IHT and sparse hidden activation recovery) are clearly described. However, the paper does not explicitly state whether code is available, which could hinder full reproduction. The dataset splits and specific implementation details for the decoding network are not fully detailed, though references to supplementary materials are provided.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-008","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper sufficiently compares against relevant prior work, including studies on CNN invertibility (Dosovitskiy and Brox, 2016; Zhang et al., 2016) and random weight CNNs (Jarrett et al., 2009; Saxe et al., 2011). The positioning of the work within the context of compressive sensing and model-based recovery is clear. However, the comparison to other theoretical models of deep learning (e.g., Arora et al., 2014; Paul and Venkatasubramanian, 2014) is limited to a brief mention in the introduction, without a detailed comparative analysis.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-031","SRC-033","SRC-037"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor is moderate. The authors report empirical model-RIP values and reconstruction errors, but do not provide confidence intervals or significance tests. The use of 1000 randomly sampled sparse activations for model-RIP evaluation is reasonable, but the lack of variance reporting limits the ability to assess the robustness of the results. The deterministic nature of the theoretical bounds reduces the need for extensive statistical testing, but empirical claims would benefit from more rigorous validation.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"a8a27c4c8cde8c914ef2a263e8aa8586e5c90a1f6fbe4f0febd7f2b73032e945","paper_id":"B1mAJI9gl","paper_ordinal":14,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"3ca39365de4079479811582f3ecb5d8090c8c3e17d2601f622a5b0d3647585f5","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"B1mAJI9gl","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"b14e3c3b987365bfa83bb134301374751e614287e173f138ee7daf45eba14cf8","request_identity_sha256":"503da5dd3930d13914be78002ef9299807ac6df71cf0f0a1ab36cf952927a801","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1026,"prompt_tokens":19275,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20301},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"503da5dd3930d13914be78002ef9299807ac6df71cf0f0a1ab36cf952927a801","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}